"[2509.13608] Is GPT-4o mini Blinded by its Own Safety Filters? Exposing the Multimodal-to-Unimodal Bottleneck in Hate Speech Detection (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13608)\nciteturn4257view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13608\",\"lineno\":10}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: This paper has been withdrawn by Niruthiha Selvanayagam\nL9: \nL10: [Submitted on 17 Sep 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 23 May 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL11: # Title:Is GPT-4o mini Blinded by its Own Safety Filters? Exposing the Multimodal-to-Unimodal Bottleneck in Hate Speech Detection\nL12: \nL13: Authors:cite6†Niruthiha Selvanayagam , cite7†Ted Kurti L14: \nL15: View a PDF of the paper titled Is GPT-4o mini Blinded by its Own Safety Filters? Exposing the Multimodal-to-Unimodal Bottleneck in Hate Speech Detection, by Niruthiha Selvanayagam and Ted Kurti\nL16: \nL17: cite8†No PDF available, click to view other formats L18: > Abstract:As Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) become integral to daily digital life, understanding their safety architectures is a critical problem for AI Alignment. This paper presents a systematic analysis of OpenAI's GPT-4o mini, a globally deployed model, on the difficult task of multimodal hate speech detection. Using the Hateful Memes Challenge dataset, we conduct a multi-phase investigation on 500 samples to probe the model's reasoning and failure modes.\nL19: Our central finding is the experimental identification of a \"Unimodal Bottleneck,\" an architectural flaw where the model's advanced multimodal reasoning is systematically preempted by context-blind safety filters. A quantitative validation of 144 content policy refusals reveals that these overrides are triggered in equal measure by unimodal visual 50% and textual 50% content.\nL20: We further demonstrate that this safety system is brittle, blocking not only high-risk imagery but also benign, common meme formats, leading to predictable false positives. These findings expose a fundamental tension between capability and safety in state-of-the-art LMMs, highlighting the need for more integrated, context-aware alignment strategies to ensure AI systems can be deployed both safely and effectively.\nL21: Comments:  | This paper reports preliminary findings from a small-scale study whose sample size is insufficient to support the stated conclusions. The authors are withdrawing it to conduct a more comprehensive evaluation\nL22: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2509.13608 [cs.LG]\nL24:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2509.13608v2 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL25:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.13608†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Niruthiha Selvanayagam [cite12†view email ]\nL29: cite5†[v1] Wed, 17 Sep 2025 00:46:42 UTC (4,147 KB)\nL30: [v2] Sat, 23 May 2026 00:20:32 UTC (1 KB) (withdrawn)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Is GPT-4o mini Blinded by its Own Safety Filters? Exposing the Multimodal-to-Unimodal Bottleneck in Hate Speech Detection, by Niruthiha Selvanayagam and Ted Kurti\nL37: \nL38:   * Withdrawn\nL39: \nL40: No license for this version due to withdrawn\nL41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.LG\nL44: \nL45: cite13†< prev |   cite14†next > L46: \nL47: cite15†new | cite16†recent | cite17†2025-09 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite18†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite19†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite20†Google Scholar†scholar.google.